 "notes": "To check: (1) Bull Sand Lightvessel (row 212) has a printed latitude of 53 54. It must be about 53 34, off Spurn. Check the scan for a printing slip or a transcription error. (2) Tees: row 222 has no name and lists the Fourth buoy, Fifth buoy and Seal Sand lights. I gave it to tees-lv, but Findlay doesn't say the No. 5 'buoy' light is a vessel. The second 1880 'Teesmouth' entry is left null because nothing identifies it. (3) Point of Ayr, Flintshire: Findlay 1887 has no row for it. The reports print 'Air, L.H.' up to 1887 next to the Dee L.V. (row 130, est. 1883). Check whether the light still stood, or whether the keepers' returns really came from somewhere else. (4) Casquets and Hanois aren't in this England extract, and Heligoland belongs to another country. All three probably have Findlay entries in other sections (Channel Islands; Germany), so look there before treating them as null. (5) Rows shared by two stations: 107 (Milford/St Ann's low, high and single-row entries) and 98 (Nash low and high). Findlay prints several lights in one row at Farne, Whitby, Spurn, Lowestoft, Orfordness, South Foreland, Lizard, Portland and Hurst, so no station needed extra_rows. (6) Names that differ: Anvil Point = DURLSTON HEAD (57); Hurst Castle = Hurst Beach (55); North Sand Head = GOODWIN LT.-VESSEL (20); East Side = EAST GOODWIN (21); Avon L.H. = row 91, not Avonmouth row 92; Falmouth Harbour = FALMOUTH (72). (7) Non-light stations left null: Great Cotes, Northrepps, Allonby, North Stack, Lundy Fog Gun (row 85 gives Lundy's position if one is wanted).",
